    "progress": [
      {
        "date": "2026-10-18",
        "text": "Mono audio devices receive the average of the stereo channels before output clipping, retaining right-only audio. Surround mixes use the same average after their ITU stereo fold-down. Regression tests cover channel balance, polarity cancellation, clipping, centered mono tracks, each 5.1 channel and several callback sizes. No readiness percentage change."
      },
      {
        "date": "2026-10-16",
        "text": "Master fader volume automation uses each render block's actual last sample, matching track faders. Breakpoints in the next block no longer pull the preceding block's level toward them, and ramps reach their last-sample value. Regression tests cover nonzero starts, one-sample callbacks and several block sizes. No readiness percentage change."
      },
      {
        "date": "2026-10-16",
        "text": "Aux, folder and master latency calculations exclude inserts already taken out for producing NaN or infinity, matching audio tracks. Subsequent blocks no longer compensate for an insert that is bypassed after failure. Regression tests check impulse timing, healthy insert latency and reset/retry at 64- and 512-sample blocks. No readiness percentage change."
      },
      {
        "date": "2026-10-14",
        "text": "MCP batch rejects more than 1000 calls before executing any command, instead of silently running only the first 1000 (#460). No readiness percentage change."
      },
      {
        "date": "2026-10-13",
        "text": "WAV: an empty data chunk followed by metadata (LIST, bext) reads as no audio; a recording that was never finalised (sizes left at 0) still gives what was written (#466)"
      },
      {
        "date": "2026-10-13",
        "text": "Sampler: a ping-pong loop keeps its phase and direction when a step is wider than the loop (a narrow loop with high transposition), instead of reflecting once and dropping the rest of the travel (#463)"
      },
      {
        "date": "2026-10-13",
        "text": "mix.instrument takes the built-in instrument's parameters in params (validated, clamped) and refuses any other field, so a synth setting written beside plugin is an error instead of being accepted and ignored (#265)"
      },
      {
        "date": "2026-10-13",
        "text": "A bounce of a bus that an aux returns onto lines up with the timeline: the aux insert's lookahead is compensated like the main mix's (it left the bus late and cut the end off), and tracks routed to that bus wait for it too (#246)"
      },
      {
        "date": "2026-10-13",
        "text": "Delay compensation covers the inserts of the master faders: a bounce of the main mix with a Maximizer on the master lines up with the same mix without it (it came out 71 samples late) (#351)"
      },
      {
        "date": "2026-10-12",
        "text": "(audio device settings): Setup › Playback Engine chooses the audio system, output and input device, sample rate and buffer size, kept per machine with the preferences; 16/32-bit integer devices are converted; ASIO is available behind the opt-in asio build feature (verified with an SSL 2+ at 128 samples). Channel routing, one duplex clock and latency compensation remain (G-01). No readiness percentage change."
      },
      {
        "date": "2026-10-12",
        "text": "(Mix in a separate window): Window › Mix in Separate Window shows the Mix window in its own native window, so Edit and Mix can be on screen at once (e.g. one per monitor), as in Pro Tools. No readiness percentage change."
      },
      {
        "date": "2026-10-12",
        "text": "(plugin editors on Windows): VST3 editors and embedded CLAP editors open in a SoundCraft host window on Windows (owned by the app window, resized on request, closed from its title bar); verified with a JUCE VST3 compressor and a CLAP effect. No readiness percentage change."
      },
      {
        "date": "2026-10-12",
        "text": "soundcraft-cli convert rejects an unparsable or zero --rate and an unknown --bit-depth (exit 1, nothing written) instead of converting with a default (#256)"
      },
      {
        "date": "2026-10-12",
        "text": "Timecode labels with huge fields no longer overflow: frame counts are computed in 128 bits and clamp, so setup.currenttimecode and session.formattime saturate instead of failing (#248)"
      },
      {
        "date": "2026-10-12",
        "text": "session.formattime with the smallest bar number no longer fails with an internal error: the bar offset saturates (#249)"
      },
      {
        "date": "2026-10-12",
        "text": "(plugin editors on Windows): VST3 editors and embedded CLAP editors open in a SoundCraft host window on Windows (owned by the app window, resized on request, closed from its title bar); verified with a JUCE VST3 compressor and a CLAP effect. No readiness percentage change."
      },
      {
        "date": "2026-10-12",
        "text": "(Mix in a separate window): Window › Mix in Separate Window shows the Mix window in its own native window, so Edit and Mix can be on screen at once (e.g. one per monitor), as in Pro Tools. No readiness percentage change."
      },
      {
        "date": "2026-10-12",
        "text": "(audio device settings): Setup › Playback Engine chooses the audio system, output and input device, sample rate and buffer size, kept per machine with the preferences; 16/32-bit integer devices are converted; ASIO is available behind the opt-in asio build feature (verified with an SSL 2+ at 128 samples). Channel routing, one duplex clock and latency compensation remain (G-01). No readiness percentage change."
      },
      {
        "date": "2026-10-10",
        "text": "(UI docking): Tracks and Groups, Clips, MIDI Editor, Memory Locations, and Undo History use movable panels. Edit and Mix retain separate arrangements; Clips and MIDI Editor belong to Edit. Transport, plug-in, and specialist utility windows retain their existing hosts. Panel moves and reset are available as UI commands, and saved UI preferences retain arrangements. Common toolbar and track record-enable buttons share interaction, accessible labels and keyboard focus through craft-ui, retaining blue tool selection and red record-arm states. No readiness percentage change."
      },
      {
        "date": "2026-10-10",
        "text": "— Stage re-normalized to pre-alpha by the core-workflow gate (interface recording, MIDI recording and session interchange fail). Full re-measure against Pro Tools Ultimate 2026.4.1 and the standard"
      },
      {
        "date": "2026-10-09",
        "text": "— Light theme and live system appearance, clip-gain fader and nudge, missing-media"
      },
      {
        "date": "2026-10-08",
        "text": "— v0.2.x–v0.3.0 released (Flatpak, AppImage zsync); VST3 quit crash fixed;"
      },
      {
        "date": "2026-10-07",
        "text": "— Repository published; About window credits; release CI on macOS runners."
      },
      {
        "date": "2026-10-06",
        "text": "— Video track, Audio Unit hosting, plugin state and editors, Renderer; Mix"
      },
      {
        "date": "2026-10-05",
        "text": "— Foundation crates, Edit and Mix windows, playback, CLI/MCP, parity report,"
      }
    ]
  },
